sortedWith returns a sorted list without changing the source. reversed on the complete comparator reverses both length ordering and the tie-break ordering, not merely one selected key.

order.reversed() takes this comparator as receiver and returns a comparator with the entire ordering reversed. Passing it to sortedWith changes both the length ordering and equal-length lexical order without modifying the input list.
```kotlin
fun main() {
    val order = compareBy<String> { it.length }.thenBy { it }
    val words = listOf("cat", "ant", "ox")
    println(words.sortedWith(order.reversed()))
}
```
Expected output: [cat, ant, ox].

